{"ts": "2026-08-17T10:34:48Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 89, "kind": "tool_use", "text": "Write", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "tool_use", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01W9GjbqfUTPnLZKCP9se5kf", "input": {"file_path": "/home/dev/projects/test-project/docs/spec.md", "content": "# Spec: Edit a task title\n\nTaiga #3\n\n## Triage\n\n**Feature** \u2014 net-new capability. Touches both `backend/` and `frontend/`,\nbut stays a single small feature: one new endpoint, one new store method, one\nnew interaction on an existing list row. Not split into sub-specs \u2014 the\nlayers here are the normal shape of a full-stack change in this app, not a\ndisproportionate one (compare Taiga #1, which was frontend-only because\nfiltering needed no server change; this one legitimately needs a write\nendpoint because the title itself is server state).\n\n## Goal\n\nLet someone fix a mistyped task title in place, without deleting and\nre-typing the whole task.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Given a task in the list, when the user activates edit mode for its title\n   (an explicit \"Edit\" trigger, exact treatment the ux-designer's call), then\n   the title becomes an editable input pre-filled with the task's current\n   title.\n2. Given a task in edit mode, when the user changes the text and confirms\n   (Save), then the task's title is updated both via a backend call and in\n   the UI, and edit mode closes showing the new title.\n3. Given a task in edit mode, when the user cancels, then edit mode closes,\n   the title reverts to its original value, and **no backend call is made**.\n4. Given a task in edit mode, when the user submits with an empty title,\n   then the edit is rejected: the task's title is **not** changed, no\n   backend call that would wipe the title succeeds, and the user sees that\n   the edit didn't go through (exact feedback \u2014 inline message vs. disabled\n   Save \u2014 is the ux-designer's call, mirroring how `AddTask` disables `Add`\n   until there's a trimmed, non-empty value).\n5. Given a task in edit mode, when the user submits a title that is only\n   whitespace, then it is treated the same as empty (criterion 4) \u2014 trimmed\n   before the empty check, mirroring `AddTask.jsx`'s existing\n   `title.trim()` guard.\n6. Backend: a new endpoint accepts a title update for an existing task,\n   reuses the existing `TaskIn` validation (`min_length=1, max_length=200`),\n   and returns the updated `Task`.\n7. Given the task id in the update request does not exist, then the backend\n   returns `404`, matching the existing `complete_task`/`delete_task`\n   pattern in `backend/app/main.py`.\n8. Given a title update request with an empty string, then the backend\n   returns `422` via the existing `TaskIn` field validation \u2014 same\n   behaviour as `create_task` today (`test_empty_title_is_rejected` already\n   covers the create path; the new route needs its own equivalent test).\n9. Editing a task's title does not change its `done` state, position in the\n   list, or any other field.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- No inline editing of `done` state here \u2014 `Complete`/`Delete` buttons and\n  their behaviour are unchanged.\n- No bulk/multi-select editing.\n- No edit history or undo beyond Cancel reverting the in-progress edit.\n- No character counter or live remaining-length UI for the existing 200-char\n  max \u2014 `AddTask` doesn't have one either, so this doesn't introduce a new\n  pattern.\n- No change to server-side whitespace handling on the **create** path\n  (`POST /api/tasks`). It has the same latent quirk today (Pydantic's\n  `min_length=1` counts raw characters, so a whitespace-only string\n  technically passes) and this spec doesn't touch it \u2014 the edit path avoids\n  the same problem by trimming client-side before submit, consistent with\n  how `AddTask` already guards `Add`.\n- No mandated keyboard shortcut (e.g. Escape-to-cancel) \u2014 a Cancel control\n  satisfies criterion 3; whether to also wire Escape is the ux-designer's\n  call, not a requirement here.\n\n## Affected areas\n\nBackend:\n- `backend/app/main.py` \u2014 new route, e.g. `PATCH /api/tasks/{task_id}`,\n  parallel in shape to `complete_task` (lines 32\u201337): look up via the store,\n  404 if missing, return the updated `Task` on success. Request body reuses\n  `TaskIn` (already has the right shape and validation \u2014 no new model\n  needed).\n- `backend/app/store.py` \u2014 new method alongside `set_done` (lines 30\u201335),\n  e.g. `update_title(task_id, title) -> Optional[Task]`, same\n  found-or-`None` pattern.\n- `backend/tests/test_tasks.py` \u2014 new tests for the route, in the same flat\n  file as the existing route tests (that's the convention actually in use\n  here \u2014 every existing route's tests live together in this one file,\n  despite the `test_<route>.py`-per-route wording in `CLAUDE.md`; matching\n  actual precedent over the literal doc).\n\nFrontend:\n- `frontend/src/api.js` \u2014 new `updateTask(id, title)` calling the new PATCH\n  endpoint, alongside the existing `completeTask`/`deleteTask` (lines\n  20\u201321).\n- `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx` \u2014 each row needs edit-mode UI: a\n  trigger to enter edit mode, an input pre-filled with the current title,\n  and Save/Cancel actions. Likely per-row local state (`useState` for\n  \"am I editing\" + \"current draft value\"), independent per `<li>`.\n- `frontend/src/App.jsx` \u2014 new `onUpdate` callback wired through `run()`\n  to `api.updateTask`, passed to `TaskList` alongside the existing\n  `onComplete`/`onDelete` (lines 51\u201356).\n- `frontend/src/App.css` \u2014 new styles for the edit input and its Save/Cancel\n  controls within a task row.\n- `e2e/test_tasks_e2e.py` \u2014 new end-to-end tests for the full edit flow\n  (enter edit, save, cancel, empty-title rejection), using the file's\n  existing `expect()`-based, no-`is_visible()` convention and its\n  `add_task`/`complete_task`-style helper functions.\n\nNot affected: `backend/app/models.py` (reusing `TaskIn` as-is),\n`frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx`, `frontend/src/components/\nTaskFilter.jsx` \u2014 filtering and adding are unrelated to this feature.\n\n## Edge cases\n\n- Whitespace-only title submitted \u2014 treated as empty, rejected (criterion\n  5).\n- Title unchanged (user opens edit, doesn't change anything, hits Save) \u2014\n  still submits and succeeds as a no-op update; no special-casing needed for\n  a 3-field in-memory store.\n- Editing a task that is already marked done \u2014 allowed; done state and title\n  are independent (criterion 9).\n- Two tasks opened for edit at the same time \u2014 allowed, no mutual-exclusion\n  requirement. Each row's edit state is independent local state, so this\n  falls out of the natural implementation rather than needing special\n  handling.\n- Title at the existing 200-character max, or a save attempt beyond it \u2014\n  same `TaskIn` validation as create applies unchanged (criterion 6).\n- Save request fails (network/server error) \u2014 follows the existing `run()`\n  pattern in `App.jsx` (lines 33\u201340): `error` state is set and the banner\n  shows, same as a failed add/complete/delete today. The task list re-syncs\n  from the server on the next successful `refresh()`.\n- Deleting a task while it's mid-edit isn't reachable through this app's own\n  UI in a way that creates a race \u2014 there's no concurrent second actor, and\n  a single user can't click both Delete and Save on the same row at once.\n  Not a case to guard against.\n\n## Open questions\n\n1. **Exact edit trigger and Save/Cancel affordance** (icon buttons vs. text\n   buttons, inline vs. replacing the row). Assumption: ux-designer decides,\n   matching this app's existing plain-button visual language (see\n   `TaskList`'s current `Done`/`Delete` buttons and `TaskFilter`'s segmented\n   control) \u2014 no new UI pattern introduced. Proceeding on this assumption;\n   it's a detail, not a decision that changes this spec's scope.\n2. **HTTP method/path for the update endpoint.** Assumption: `PATCH\n   /api/tasks/{task_id}`, since it's a partial update of one field on an\n   existing resource \u2014 matches REST convention and sits naturally next to\n   the existing `POST /api/tasks/{task_id}/done` and `DELETE\n   /api/tasks/{task_id}` in `main.py`. Proceeding on this assumption.\n"}, "session_id": "7316b1e8-1a1b-45f3-8c7b-90f2dd28a686"}}
